{"qid":"Q1064034","names":["Gutian Dynasty"],"title":"Gutian Dynasty","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Gutian dynasty was a line of foreign rulers from the Zagros highlands who dominated lower Mesopotamia following the collapse of the Akkadian Empire, c. 2154 BCE. The Sumerian King List records 23 Gutian kings reigning for approximately 125 years, though the list conflates parallel regional powers and the exact chronology remains contested; many rulers are attested only epigraphically. Control was never complete—major Sumerian cities retained local administrations—and the last Gutian king, Tirigan, was overthrown after a reign of only 40 days by Utu-hengal of Uruk, whose victory inscription describes Tirigan fleeing on foot before capture. The expulsion of the Gutians opened the way for the Third Dynasty of Ur.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Gutian territorial control is poorly attested; no administrative archives survive from a Gutian center, and reconstructions depend on fragmentary royal inscriptions and the ideologically charged Sumerian King List.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Gutian dynasty of Sumer","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gutian_dynasty_of_Sumer"},{"label":"Britannica: Gutian","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Gutian"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Utu-hengal","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Utu-hengal"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Gutian rule in Mesopotamia","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Erridupizir","nativeName":"","roman":"Erridupizir","start":-2147,"end":-2138,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Imta","native